{#ch10-0001}
# ALVISSMOL

{#ch10-0002}
## The Ballad of Alvis

{#ch10-0003}
## Introductory Note

{#ch10-0004}
No better summary of the Alvissmol can be given than Gering’s statement that “it is a versified chapter from the skaldic Poetics.” The narrative skeleton, contained solely in stanzas 1–8 and in 35, is of the slightest; the dwarf Alvis, desirous of marrying Thor’s daughter, is compelled by the god to answer a number of questions to test his knowledge. That all his answers are quite satisfactory makes no difference whatever to the outcome. The questions and answers differ radically from those of the {_Vafthruthnismol_}. Instead of being essentially mythological, they all concern synonyms. Thor asks what the earth, the sky, the moon, and so on, are called “in each of all the worlds,” but there is no apparent significance in the fact that the gods call the earth one thing and the giants call it another; the answers are simply strings of poetic circumlocutions, or “kennings.” Concerning the use of these “kennings” in skaldic poetry, cf. introductory note to the {_Hymiskvitha_}.

{#ch10-0005}
Mogk is presumably right in dating the poem as late as the twelfth century, assigning it to the period of “the Icelandic renaissance of skaldic poetry.” It appears to have been the work of a man skilled in poetic construction,—Thor’s questions, for instance, are neatly balanced in pairs,—and fully familiar with the intricacies of skaldic diction, but distinctly weak in his mythology. In other words, it is learned rather than spontaneous poetry. Finnur Jonsson’s attempt to make it a tenth century Norwegian poem baffles logic. Vigfusson is pretty sure the poem shows marked traces of Celtic influence, which is by no means incompatible with Mogk’s theory (cf. introductory note to the {_Rigsthula_}).

{#ch10-0006}
The poem is found only in {_Regius_}, where it follows the {_Thrymskvitha_}. Snorri quotes stanzas 20 and 30, the manuscripts of the {_Prose Edda_} giving the name of the poem as {_Alvissmol_}, {_Alsvinnsmol_} or {_Olvismol_}. It is apparently in excellent condition, without serious errors of transmission, although interpolations or omissions in such a poem might have been made so easily as to defy detection.

{#ch10-0007}
The translation of the many synonyms presents, of course, []{.pb n="184"}unusual difficulties, particularly as many of the Norse words can be properly rendered in English only by more or less extended phrases. I have kept to the original meanings as closely as I could without utterly destroying the metrical structure.

{#ch10-0008}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0009 ref="alv.1"}
::: stanza
“Now shall the bride | my benches adorn,
And homeward haste forthwith;
Eager for wedlock | to all shall I seem,
Nor at home shall they rob me of rest.”
:::

{#ch10-0010}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0011 ref="alv.2"}
::: stanza
“What, pray, art thou? | Why so pale round the nose?
By the dead hast thou lain of late?
To a giant like | dost thou look, methinks;
Thou wast not born for the bride.”
:::

{#ch10-0012}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0013 ref="alv.3"}
::: stanza
“Alvis am I, | and under the earth
My home ’neath the rocks I have;[]{.pb n="185"}
With the wagon-guider | a word do I seek;
Let the gods their bond not break.”
:::

{#ch10-0014}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0015 ref="alv.4"}
::: stanza
“Break it shall I, | for over the bride
Her father has foremost right;
At home was I not | when the promise thou hadst,
And I give her alone of the gods.”
:::

{#ch10-0016}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0017 ref="alv.5"}
::: stanza
“What hero claims | such right to hold
O’er the bride that shines so bright?
Not many will know thee, | thou wandering man\!
Who was bought with rings to bear thee?”
:::

{#ch10-0018}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0019 ref="alv.6"}
::: stanza
“Vingthor, the wanderer | wide, am I,
And I am Sithgrani’s son;
Against my will | shalt thou get the maid,
And win the marriage word.”
:::

{#ch10-0020}
[]{.pb n="186"}{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0021 ref="alv.7"}
::: stanza
“Thy good-will now | shall I quickly get,
And win the marriage word;
I long to have, | and I would not lack,
This snow-white maid for mine.”
:::

{#ch10-0022}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0023 ref="alv.8"}
::: stanza
“The love of the maid | I may not keep thee
From winning, thou guest so wise,
If of every world | thou canst tell me all
That now I wish to know.
:::

{#ch10-0024 ref="alv.9"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the earth, | that lies before all,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0025}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0026 ref="alv.10"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Earth’ to men, ‘Field’ | to the gods it is,
‘The Ways’ is it called by the Wanes;[]{.pb n="187"}
‘Ever Green’ by the giants, | ‘The Grower’ by elves,
‘The Moist’ by the holy ones high.”
:::

{#ch10-0027}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0028 ref="alv.11"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the heaven, | beheld of the high one,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0029}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0030 ref="alv.12"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Heaven’ men call it, | ‘The Height’ the gods,
The Wanes ‘The Weaver of Winds’;
Giants ‘The Up-World,’ | elves ‘The Fair-Roof,’
The dwarfs ‘The Dripping Hall.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0031}
[]{.pb n="188"}{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0032 ref="alv.13"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the moon, | that men behold,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0033}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0034 ref="alv.14"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Moon’ with men, ‘Flame’ | the gods among,
‘The Wheel’ in the house of hell;
‘The Goer’ the giants, | ‘The Gleamer’ the dwarfs,
The elves ‘The Teller of Time.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0035}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0036 ref="alv.15"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the sun, | that all men see,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0037}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0038 ref="alv.16"}
::: stanza
“Men call it ‘Sun,’ | gods ‘Orb of the Sun,’
‘The Deceiver of Dvalin’ the dwarfs;
The giants ‘The Ever-Bright,’ | elves ‘Fair Wheel,’
‘All-Glowing’ the sons of the gods.”
:::

{#ch10-0039}
[]{.pb n="189"}{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0040 ref="alv.17"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the clouds, | that keep the rains,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0041}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0042 ref="alv.18"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Clouds’ men name them, | ‘Rain-Hope’ gods call them,
The Wanes call them ‘Kites of the Wind’;
‘Water-Hope’ giants, | ‘Weather-Might’ elves,
‘The Helmet of Secrets’ in hell.”
:::

{#ch10-0043}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0044 ref="alv.19"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the wind, | that widest fares,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0045}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0046 ref="alv.20"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Wind’ do men call it, | the gods ‘The Waverer,’
‘The Neigher’ the holy ones high;[]{.pb n="190"}
‘The Wailer’ the giants, | ‘Roaring Wender’ the elves,
In hell ‘The Blustering Blast.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0047}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0048 ref="alv.21"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the calm, | that quiet lies,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0049}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0050 ref="alv.22"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Calm’ men call it, | ‘The Quiet’ the gods,
The Wanes ‘The Hush of the Winds’;
‘The Sultry’ the giants, | elves ‘Day’s Stillness,’
The dwarfs ‘The Shelter of Day.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0051}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0052 ref="alv.23"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the sea, | whereon men sail,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0053}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0054 ref="alv.24"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Sea’ men call it, | gods ‘The Smooth-Lying,’
‘The Wave’ is it called by the Wanes;[]{.pb n="191"}
‘Eel-Home’ the giants, | ‘Drink-Stuff’ the elves,
For the dwarfs its name is ‘The Deep.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0055}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0056 ref="alv.25"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the fire, | that flames for men,
In each of all the worlds?”
:::

{#ch10-0057}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0058 ref="alv.26"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Fire’ men call it, | and ‘Flame’ the gods,
By the Wanes is it ‘Wildfire’ called;
‘The Biter’ by giants, | ‘The Burner’ by dwarfs,
‘The Swift’ in the house of hell.”
:::

{#ch10-0059}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0060 ref="alv.27"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the wood, | that grows for mankind,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0061}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0062 ref="alv.28"}
::: stanza
“Men call it ‘The Wood,’ | gods ‘The Mane of the Field,’[]{.pb n="192"}
‘Seaweed of Hills’ in hell;
‘Flame-Food’ the giants, | ‘Fair-Limbed’ the elves,
‘The Wand’ is it called by the Wanes.”
:::

{#ch10-0063}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0064 ref="alv.29"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the night, | the daughter of Nor,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0065}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0066 ref="alv.30"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Night’ men call it, | ‘Darkness’ gods name it,
‘The Hood’ the holy ones high;
The giants ‘The Lightless,’ | the elves ‘Sleep’s Joy,’
The dwarfs ‘The Weaver of Dreams.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0067}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0068 ref="alv.31"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the seed, | that is sown by men,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0069}
[]{.pb n="193"}{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0070 ref="alv.32"}
::: stanza
“Men call it ‘Grain,’ | and ‘Corn’ the gods,
‘Growth’ in the world of the Wanes;
‘The Eaten’ by giants, | ‘Drink-Stuff’ by elves,
In hell ‘The Slender Stem.’ ”
:::

{#ch10-0071}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0072 ref="alv.33"}
::: stanza
“Answer me, Alvis\! | thou knowest all,
Dwarf, of the doom of men:
What call they the ale, | that is quaffed of men,
In each and every world?”
:::

{#ch10-0073}
{_Alvis spake_}:

{#ch10-0074 ref="alv.34"}
::: stanza
“ ‘Ale’ among men, | ‘Beer’ the gods among,
In the world of the Wanes ‘The Foaming’;
‘Bright Draught’ with giants, | ‘Mead’ with dwellers in hell,
‘The Feast-Draught’ with Suttung’s sons.”
:::

{#ch10-0075}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch10-0076 ref="alv.35"}
::: stanza
“In a single breast | I never have seen
More wealth of wisdom old;[]{.pb n="194"}
But with treacherous wiles | must I now betray thee:
The day has caught thee, dwarf\!
(Now the sun shines here in the hall.)”
:::

{#ch10-0077 on="alv.1"}
::::: commentary
[]{.pb n="183"}[]{.pb n="184"}{_Alvis_} (“All-Knowing”): a dwarf, not elsewhere mentioned. The manuscript nowhere indicates the speakers’ names. The bride in question is Thor’s daughter; Thruth (“Might”) is the only daughter of his whose name is recorded, and she does not appear elsewhere in the poems. Her mother was Sif, Thor’s wife, whereas the god’s sons were born of a giantess. {_Benches_}: cf. {_Lokasenna_}, 15 and note.
:::::

{#ch10-0078 on="alv.2"}
::::: commentary
The dwarfs, living beyond the reach of the sun, which was fatal to them (cf. stanzas 16 and 35), were necessarily pale. Line 3 is, of course, ironical.
:::::

{#ch10-0079 on="alv.3"}
::::: commentary
{_Wagon-guider_}: Thor, who travels habitually on his goat-drawn wagon. Bugge changes “Vagna vers” to “Vapna verþs,” []{.pb n="185"}rendering the line “I am come to seek   \|   the cost of the weapons.” In either case, Alvis does not as yet recognize Thor.
:::::

{#ch10-0080 on="alv.4"}
::::: commentary
Apparently the gods promised Thor’s daughter in marriage to Alvis during her father’s absence, perhaps as a reward for some craftsmanship of his (cf. Bugge’s suggestion as to stanza 3). The text of line 4 is most uncertain.
:::::

{#ch10-0081 on="alv.5"}
::::: commentary
{_Hero_}: ironically spoken; Alvis takes Thor for a tramp, the god’s uncouth appearance often leading to such mistakes; cf. {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 6. Line 4 is a trifle uncertain; some editors alter the wording to read “What worthless woman bore thee?”
:::::

{#ch10-0082 on="alv.6"}
::::: commentary
{_Vingthor_} (“Thor the Hurler”): cf. {_Thrymskvitha_}, 1. {_Sithgrani_} (“Long-Beard”): Othin.[]{.pb n="186"}
:::::

{#ch10-0083 on="alv.8"}
::::: commentary
{_Every world_}: concerning the nine worlds, cf. {_Voluspo_}, 2 and note. Many editors follow this stanza with one spoken by Alvis, found in late paper manuscripts, as follows: “Ask then, Vingthor,   \|   since eager thou art / The lore of the dwarf to learn; / Oft have I fared   \|   in the nine worlds all, / And wide is my wisdom of each.”
:::::

{#ch10-0084 on="alv.10"}
::::: commentary
{_Men_}, etc.: nothing could more clearly indicate the author’s mythological inaccuracy than his confusion of the inhabitants of the nine worlds. Men (dwellers in Mithgarth) appear in each of Alvis’s thirteen answers; so do the gods (Asgarth) and the giants (Jotunheim). The elves (Alfheim) appear in eleven []{.pb n="187"}answers, the Wanes (Vanaheim) in nine, and the dwarfs (who occupied no special world, unless one identifies them with the dark elves of Svartalfaheim) in seven. The dwellers “in hell” appear in six stanzas; the phrase probably refers to the world of the dead, though Mogk thinks it may mean the dwarfs. In stanzas where the gods are already listed appear names elsewhere applied only to them,—“holy ones,” “sons of the gods” and “high ones,”—as if these names meant beings of a separate race. “Men” appears twice in the same stanza, and so do the giants, if one assumes that they are “the sons of Suttung.” Altogether it is useless to pay much attention to the mythology of Alvis’s replies.
:::::

{#ch10-0085 on="alv.11"}
::::: commentary
Lines 1, 2, and 4 of Thor’s questions are regularly abbreviated in the manuscript. {_Beheld_}, etc.: the word in the manuscript is almost certainly an error, and all kinds of guesses have been made to rectify it. All that can be said is that it means “beheld of” or “known to” somebody.[]{.pb n="188"}
:::::

{#ch10-0086 on="alv.14"}
::::: commentary
{_Flame_}: a doubtful word; Vigfusson suggests that it properly means a “mock sun.” {_Wheel_}: the manuscript adds the adjective “whirling,” to the destruction of the metre; cf. {_Hovamol_}, 84, 3.
:::::

{#ch10-0087 on="alv.16"}
::::: commentary
{_Deceiver of Dvalin_}: Dvalin was one of the foremost dwarfs; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 14, {_Fafnismol_}, 13, and {_Hovamol_}, 144. The []{.pb n="189"}sun “deceives” him because, like the other dwarfs living underground, he cannot live in its light, and always fears lest sunrise may catch him unaware. The sun’s rays have power to turn the dwarfs into stone, and the giantess Hrimgerth meets a similar fate (cf. {_Helgakvitha Hjorvarthssonar_}, 30). Alvis suffers in the same way; cf. stanza 35.
:::::

{#ch10-0088 on="alv.20"}
::::: commentary
Snorri quotes this stanza in the {_Skaldskaparmal_}. {_Waverer_}: the word is uncertain, the {_Prose Edda_} manuscripts giving it in various forms. {_Blustering Blast_}: two {_Prose Edda_} manuscripts give a totally different word, meaning “The Pounder.”[]{.pb n="190"}
:::::

{#ch10-0089 on="alv.22"}
::::: commentary
{_Hush_}, etc.: the manuscript, by inserting an additional letter, makes the word practically identical with that translated “Kite” in stanza 18. Most editors have agreed as to the emendation.
:::::

{#ch10-0090 on="alv.24"}
::::: commentary
{_Drink-Stuff_}: Gering translates the word thus; I doubt it, but can suggest nothing better.[]{.pb n="191"}
:::::

{#ch10-0091 on="alv.26"}
::::: commentary
{_Wildfire_}: the word may mean any one of various things, including “Wave,” which is not unlikely.
:::::

{#ch10-0092 on="alv.28"}
::::: commentary
{_In hell_}: the word simply means “men,” and it is only a guess, though a generally accepted one, that here it refers to the dead.[]{.pb n="192"}
:::::

{#ch10-0093 on="alv.29"}
::::: commentary
{_Nor_}: presumably the giant whom Snorri calls Norvi or Narfi, father of Not (Night) and grandfather of Dag (Day). Cf. {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 25.
:::::

{#ch10-0094 on="alv.30"}
::::: commentary
Snorri quotes this stanza in the {_Skaldskaparmal_}. The various {_Prose Edda_} manuscripts differ considerably in naming the gods, the giants, etc. {_Lightless_}: some manuscripts have “The Unsorrowing.”
:::::

{#ch10-0095 on="alv.32"}
::::: commentary
{_Grain_}: the two words translated “grain” and “corn” apparently both meant primarily barley, and thence grain in []{.pb n="193"}general, the first being the commoner term of the two. {_Drink-Stuff_}: the word is identical with the one used, and commented on, in stanza 24, and again I have followed Gering’s interpretation for want of a better one. If his guess is correct, the reference here is evidently to grain as the material from which beer and other drinks are brewed.
:::::

{#ch10-0096 on="alv.34"}
::::: commentary
{_Suttung’s sons_}: these ought to be the giants, but the giants are specifically mentioned in line 3. The phrase “Suttung’s sons” occurs in {_Skirnismol_}, 34, clearly meaning the giants. Concerning Suttung as the possessor of the mead of poetry, cf. {_Hovamol_}, 104.[]{.pb n="194"}
:::::

{#ch10-0097 on="alv.35"}
::::: commentary
Concerning the inability of the dwarfs to endure sunlight, which turns them into stone, cf. stanza 16 and note. Line 5 may be spurious.[]{.pb n="195"}
:::::

